Run on prod (2026-09-04): 0 scored of 363. Every row came back partial with
probe_budget_unreconstructable, because probeColdStart is ON there, so
probeGateFor runs for every decision and downgrades every one. The budget
half of that gate genuinely cannot be rebuilt — task status is current
state, not history — and the first version let that fact taint rows it could
not possibly affect. The design failed on first contact exactly as its own §7
said to check for, and gapCounts named the assumption that broke.

demandAllocation reads probeAllowed on two branches only: no market data
with a sales boost under the half tier, and a sub-floor score with at most
probeMaxSellers rival sellers. Everywhere else the value is dead, so not
knowing it is provably irrelevant and the row is exact — the harness's own
§3 rule, which it had applied to the sales price and to cold-start-off but
not to this case. Measured on the 363 prod rows: 53 no snapshot
(unreplayable, correct), 27 probe-reachable (partial, correct), 283 with
research, sellers > probeMaxSellers and d below the half tier — 78% — which
should be exact and now are.

Decided by asking the economics rather than restating them.
probeGateLoadBearing() runs demandAllocation with probeAllowed true and again
with it false and compares the outputs; identical means the unknown budget
cannot touch the row. Restating the two branch conditions here would have put
DEMAND_HALF and the boost formula in a second place, to drift the first time
the tiers moved. Each row now carries probeGateMatters (true / false / null)
so the split is auditable in the JSON output.

Six new tests. The pinned one: research present, sellers > probeMaxSellers,
score below the half tier, probeColdStart ON → exact. The two probe-reachable
branches stay partial. The summary scores the exact rows and no longer lists
the gap for them.

Suite 665/670, same 5 pre-existing orphan-test failures. No change to
autoFarmer.js or autoLister.js in this commit.
